-------------------------------------------------------------------

Four deltas were given in the dispatch and all four were re-measured here, not assumed.

**3a. The group A 802.1X rig is GONE.** T9032 run 2 (2026-10-28, commit `f154abc`) ran group
A's restore recipe: `radius-server host`/`radius-server local`, `aaa authentication dot1x`,
`dot1x port-control` on port1.0.48 and the four local RADIUS users are all removed. Confirmed
here in §5: the pre-run `show running-config` has no `radius-server` line of any kind and no
`dot1x` line. **So N12 — "`radius-server local` withdrawn by secure mode" — had nothing to
withdraw.** What run 2 did about that is in §6f and §7-N12: a **minimal local RADIUS server was
rebuilt** (server + one NAS + one user), which gives N12 a real subject at the wire level via
`radsend.py`. The **802.1X half** of N12 (a supplicant on port1.0.48 that authenticated before
and should fail after) was deliberately **not** rebuilt — see §7-N12 for the split.

**3b. One piece of PKI state is pre-existing residue, not secure mode's doing.** The enrolment
`cktls` under trustpoint `local` was created by T9032 run 1; there is no CLI to un-enroll on
this build and removing it would mean deleting trustpoint `local`, which the queue forbids.
**A FRESH PKI inventory was therefore taken before the enable** (§5d) and that fresh inventory —
not run 1's five-day-old §6.6d copy — is what N11 compares against.

**3c. swi_d carries a dirty health baseline** — two unattributed unexpected reboots and an
snmpd SIGSEGV from the morning of 2026-10-23 (UTC 2026-10-22 23:49–23:55). Every case that week
graded CLEAN *against* that baseline. Run 2 adds deliberate reboots of its own, so §12 separates
the reboots this run caused from anything it did not.

**3d. The bench starts clean.** The pre-run probe is exit **0 MATCH** (§4), where run 1's was
exit 2 NEEDS-CHECK with two `CHECK_CABLE` lines. Both have cleared: T9032 run 2's traffic
refreshed the box MAC in the FDB on both eth3/port1.0.10 and eth4/port1.0.48.


