7. THE MEASUREMENTS — N1 to N15, each with its own verdict and its own evidence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### N1 — the enable step itself, and the device's response — MEASURED

`crypto secure-mode` typed in global configuration mode at 09:50:04. The device's **entire**
response, verbatim, read before anything was answered:

    awplus(config)#crypto secure-mode
    % Save the config and restart the system for this change to take effect.

🔴 **There is NO (y/n) prompt.** Run 1's recovery plan told the attended run to read the prompt
before answering; there is nothing to answer. The command is accepted on the spot and the one
`% ` line is an instruction, not an error. Immediately afterwards:

    awplus#show secure-mode
    Secure mode is enabled
    awplus#show running-config | include secure
    crypto secure-mode
    awplus#show startup-config | include secure
    (nothing)

### N2 — does it reboot, on its own or by prompting? — MEASURED. **U1 ANSWERED: NO.**

It does **not** reboot on its own and it does **not** prompt. Uptime was still
`4 days 20:56:38` at 09:52:24, three minutes after the enable. The change needs an explicit
`write` and `reload`, both of which the tester must issue:

    awplus#write
    Building configuration...
    [OK]
    awplus#show startup-config | include secure
    crypto secure-mode

`reload` → `reboot system? (y/n): y` → **login seen after 61 s**. After the restart
`show system` read `Uptime : 0 days 00:01:06` and `show secure-mode` read `Secure mode is
enabled`. `show boot` was unchanged throughout: `Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg`.

🔴 **FINDING F-1 — `show secure-mode` reads "enabled" before secure mode is in force.** The
device says in one breath that the change needs a save and a restart, and in the next that
secure mode is enabled. That is the `platforms/x960.md` §5 / §7F.2 family: the `show` command
describes the configuration, not the enforced state. It matters because **the enforcement in
that window is partial**, which §N3 measures. A case graded from `show secure-mode` alone would
believe the whole feature was active when most of it was not.

### N3 — what the enable does to startup-config, and whether disabling restores it — MEASURED. **U2 ANSWERED.**

Three distinct phases, and they are not the same:

**(i) At enable time, before any restart.** Running-config changed in exactly two places
(full diff, `work/rc-presave.out` against `work/rc-before-run2.out`):

    -service telnet
    -no service telnet ipv6
    +no service telnet
    +crypto secure-mode

Nothing else. The v2c community, the SNMPv3 user, `username ckp7 privilege 7`, the NTP MD5 key
and the whole `radius-server local` block were all **still present**. And the enforcement
matched: telnet TCP/23 was already refused (`[Errno 111] Connection refused`) while SNMPv2c
still returned a value and the local RADIUS server still returned Access-Accept. The **CLI
parser was completely unrestricted** — `service telnet ?`, `username test7 privilege ?`
(`<0-15>`) and `snmp-server user ... auth ?` (md5 present) were byte-identical to normal mode.

**(ii) At the restart.** The device **announced** every line it refused rather than dropping
them silently. On the console during boot:

    WARNING: Failed to execute the following commands:
    username ckp7 privilege 7 password 8 $5$o8/iulS8Cg5389J0$UEXNLIlOLKEAr9Bx4ZhnAfgMBVTie4G1O9enTKrDMcA
    snmp-server community cktest
    snmp-server user ckv3 ckgrp encrypted auth sha 0x05de9451cf93eb8a79948bb495e765630266332868 priv aes 0x059c399342e8393e909b5b05bc9f55ed2c81fe7247
    radius-server local
     server enable
     nas 10.38.22.33 key +gqfn7cOnp83ulC8akPX/9XuVpItdisbnaoeOBiSfYs= encrypted
     user ckrad encrypted password uV3Hzwc1fkxN/uN2+d+U2VDuO00NGvoIguLbH8XYLt4=
    ntp authentication-key 10 md5 ckntpkey1

and in `show log`, one `local6.warning` line per rejection **with the startup-config line number
and the reason** — independent confirmation of N5, N6 and N7 from the device's own log:

    startup-config: 7: username ckp7 privilege 7 password 8 $5$... -- Privilege level 15
    startup-config: 22: snmp-server community cktest  -- Community is not allowed in crypto secure-mode
    startup-config: 23: snmp-server user ckv3 ckgrp encrypted auth sha 0x05de... -- SHA-1 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode

`service telnet` is **absent** from that list, because it had already been rewritten to
`no service telnet` in phase (i). Consistent, and worth knowing.

**(iii) Disabling.** `no crypto secure-mode` gives the **same** advisory
(`% Save the config and restart the system for this change to take effect.`), `show secure-mode`
flips to `disabled` at once, and after `write` + `reload` the line is gone from both configs.
🔴 **The rejected lines do NOT come back.** They were dropped from the saved configuration at
phase (ii), so a `write` in secure mode makes the loss permanent. **That is the single most
important operational consequence of this case** and it is why §5a's `pre-securemode.cfg` was
taken before anything else.

### N4 — telnet refusal (title clause 1) — **PASS** for the server, **FINDING** for the client

Graded on the wire, never on `show telnet` (`platforms/x960.md` §7F.2 forbids the latter).

| leg | normal mode | secure mode |
| --- | --- | --- |
| telnet **server**, TCP/23 from the box (`netlogin.py`) | `LOGIN RESULT: USER proto=telnet seconds=0.2`, session returned `Secure mode is disabled` | `TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.34:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused` |
| `service telnet ip` accepted? | yes (with the §7F.3 advisory) | `% Action not allowed in crypto-secure mode.` |
| running-config | `service telnet` + `no service telnet ipv6` | rewritten to `no service telnet` at enable time |

A TCP RST, not a timeout — the server is gone, not firewalled. **PASS.**

🔴 **FINDING F-2 — CR-84010 is NOT implemented on this build: the telnet CLIENT still works in
secure mode.** The wiki row says *"CR-84010: telnet client is disabled"*. Driven with
`devclient.py` from the DUT's own console at 10:02, with secure mode enabled:

    awplus#telnet ip 10.38.22.33
    % Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated. Please use SSH.
    telnet: Unable to connect to remote host (10.38.22.33): Connection refused
    CLIENT RESULT: REFUSED  proto=telnet  target=10.38.22.33  seconds=5.7

The refusal came from the **far end** (the testbox runs no telnet server) — i.e. the DUT's
client ran, resolved, opened a socket and received a RST. A disabled client would have been
refused by the DUT's own CLI instead. `telnet ?` in exec also still offers
`WORD / ip / ipv6 / vrf` in secure mode. The client is alive.

### N5 — SNMPv1/v2c disabled, SNMPv3 only (title clause 2) — **PASS** on the negative half; the positive half **NOT MEASURABLE**

| leg | normal mode | secure mode |
| --- | --- | --- |
| v2c GET, community `cktest` (`snmpq.py`) | `RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0 = STRING: Allied Telesis router/switch, Software (AlliedWare Plus) Version sin_x96056-20261019-27` (exit 0) | `RESULT TIMEOUT -- Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.34.` (exit 4) |
| `snmp-server community cktest ro` | accepted | `% Community is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
| the configured community | present in running-config | removed at the restart, logged by line number |
| `snmp-server user ... auth md5 ... priv des ...` | accepted | `% MD5 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
| `snmp-server user ... auth sha ... priv aes ...` | accepted | 🔴 `% SHA-1 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
| `snmp-server user ... auth sha-256 ... priv aes ...` | n/a | **accepted**, stored encrypted in running-config |

**PASS** on the title's claim: SNMPv1/v2c is genuinely disabled, proved by a GET that returned a
value before and timed out after, with the community gone from the configuration.

🔴 **FINDING F-3 — secure mode is STRICTER than the wiki says: SHA-1 is refused too.** The wiki
row says only *"MD5 and DES are not allowed for authentication"*. On this build **SHA-1 is also
refused**, by name, and only **SHA-256** is accepted. That is why the SNMPv3 user configured in
normal mode with `auth sha` was thrown out at the restart (§N3 ii) although it used no MD5 and
no DES. Anyone reading the wiki would expect that user to survive. It does not.

**NOT RUN — DES as a privacy protocol on its own.** The `auth md5 ... priv des ...` line was
rejected on its auth keyword, so the parser never evaluated `priv des`. DES-alone was not
retested with an accepted auth protocol.

**NOT MEASURABLE — "an SNMPv3 request still succeeds under secure mode", from this box.** Two
independent instrument limits, both measured, neither a DUT property:
- tb277's net-snmp is **5.7.3**, whose `-a` accepts only `MD5|SHA`:
  `Invalid authentication protocol specified after -a flag: SHA-256`;
- pysnmp **is installed** but unusable — `import pysnmp.hlapi` raises
  `OSError: Cannot load native module 'Cryptodome.Cipher._raw_ecb'`, so
  `usmHMAC192SHA256AuthProtocol` is unreachable (probed on the box, 10:02).

Secure mode refuses both protocols the box can speak, so **tb277 cannot drive an SNMPv3 query
against swi_d in secure mode at all.** Fixing either needs a package install, i.e. root, i.e.
the Test Engineer (STANDING-ORDERS §4). ⚠️ **This is an instrument limitation on tb277, NOT a
DUT result. The DUT ACCEPTED the SHA-256/AES user at the CLI and stored it** — that is the
strongest statement this bench supports, and it is a positive one.

⚠️ **TOOL DEFECT found here, recorded and deliberately NOT fixed mid-run** (the sentinel will
raise it separately so no edit lands under a case that is using the tool). Given
`--auth-proto SHA-256`, `tools/snmpq.py` passed it to net-snmp, net-snmp rejected the flag and
printed its **usage text**, and snmpq.py parsed a line out of that usage dump and reported
`RESULT VALUE -- v:  print values only (not OID = value)` **with exit 0**. A usage error graded
as a successful SNMP GET. Any case using snmpq.py with a protocol name the local net-snmp does
not know would record a **false PASS**.

### N6 — privilege levels 1 and 15 only (title clause 3) — **PASS**; the password-length half NOT DISCRIMINABLE

    awplus(config)#username ckp7 privilege 7 password ckPriv7pass
    % Only the following privilege levels are allowed in crypto secure-mode:
    % Privilege level 1
    % Privilege level 15

Control, same console batch, two seconds later: `username ckp1 privilege 1 password ckPriv1pass`
was **accepted** and appears in running-config. And the pre-existing privilege-7 user was thrown
out at the restart with the device's own reason (`-- Privilege level 15`, §N3 ii). The same
command was accepted without comment in normal mode (§6c). **PASS**, on the pair.

⚠️ **NOT DISCRIMINABLE — the "`enable password` must be ≥ 8 characters" half.**
`enable password short1` returned `% Password must be at least 8 characters` in secure mode, but
this unit already carried **`security-password minimum-length 8`** in its pre-case baseline
(§5f), which produces the same refusal in normal mode. The refusal cannot be attributed to
secure mode on this unit. Clearing `security-password minimum-length` to separate them would
weaken a standing security setting on a shared bench; it was not done.

### N7 — weak cryptographic algorithms (title clause 4) — **SPLIT**

| limb | result |
| --- | --- |
| NTP MD5 key | **PASS.** `ntp authentication-key 10 md5 ckntpkey1` → `% Command is not allowed when crypto secure-mode is enabled.` Accepted in normal mode (§6d), and the existing key was thrown out at the restart |
| NTP SHA1 control | **PASS.** `ntp authentication-key 11 sha1 ckntpkey2` **accepted** in secure mode, stored as `ntp authentication-key 11 sha1-encrypted ...`. Matches the wiki (MD5 out, SHA1 in) |
| `allow-legacy-ssh-rsa` | **PASS.** `% allow-legacy-ssh-rsa is not supported in crypto secure-mode.` |
| `ssh server disallow-cbc-ciphers` | **PASS.** Accepted in secure mode, as the wiki says it should be — the one SSH cipher control that remains |
| rsa1 and dsa hostkeys | 🔴 **NOT DISCRIMINABLE** — see below |

🔴 **FINDING F-4 — the "rsa1 and dsa keys are not supported in secure-mode" row cannot be
confirmed on this platform, because they are not supported in NORMAL mode either.**
`crypto key generate hostkey ?` offers **ecdsa, ed25519, rsa and nothing else**, identically in
both modes (§6e, and re-probed at 09:56 in secure mode). There is no rsa1 and no dsa keyword to
withdraw. The one place those keywords DO appear is the **destroy** path, and they appear there
**in secure mode too**:

    awplus(config)#crypto key destroy userkey ckuser ?
      dsa      DSA crypto algorithm
      ecdsa    ECDSA crypto algorithm
      ed25519  ED25519 crypto algorithm
      rsa      RSA crypto algorithm
      rsa1     SSHv1 public key

So the title's "RSA1, DSA" clause is satisfied on this unit **at all times**, which is a
different and weaker statement than "secure mode disables them". Only the normal-mode baseline
of §6e could show that, and it is the reason that baseline was taken.

### N8 — `show running-config unencrypted` refused — **PASS**

    normal mode : returned the whole configuration (§6h)
    secure mode : % Command not supported in secure-mode

🔴 Part of **FINDING F-5** (below): `show running-config ?` in secure mode **still lists**
`unencrypted  Show passwords and keys in cleartext`.

### N9 — NVS unreachable from IMISH — **PASS**

    normal mode : dir nvs:  ->  full listing (dhcp/, reboot.log, fsck.log, chrony.drf, ...)
    secure mode : dir nvs:  ->  % Invalid URL: nvs:
    after disable: dir nvs: ->  full listing again

Three-point arc, so the restriction is shown to be applied **and** released. This unit has real
NVS, so the restriction is the real one, not the `/flash/.nvs` variant.

### N10 — `show post` crypto info, and `crypto secure-mode-selftest` — 🔴 **FAIL (product finding). THE OPEN QUESTION IS SETTLED.**

The dispatch's one open question was whether `crypto secure-mode-selftest` registers once secure
mode is on. **It does not.** Probed read-only with `qmark` at 09:55, with secure mode enabled:

    awplus#crypto secure-mode-selftest ?
    % Unrecognized command

    awplus#crypto ?
      key          Key management
      pki          Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) Commands
      random       Generate a random byte sequence
      secure-mode  Secure mode

Four sub-commands — **the same four as in normal mode**. Nothing new registers. So the command
is **unimplemented on the x960 on `sin_x96056-20261019-27`**, not secure-mode-gated. Run 1 could
not separate those two readings; this settles it.

🔴 **FINDING F-6 — and the other documented route is empty too.** The wiki promises that in
secure mode `show post` gives *"results from post and cryptographic algorithms/modules"*. It
does add a heading, and the heading has **nothing under it**. From the box-side transcript
(`/tmp/secmode2/console-u2.log`), not from a tool's stdout:

    POST - Power on self test
    -------------------------

    Mounting static filesystems... OK
    Checking flash filesystem... OK
    Mounting flash filesystem (ext3)... OK
    Checking NVS filesystem... OK
    Mounting NVS filesystem... OK
    Checking system reboot stability... OK

    SECURE-MODE POST
    ---------------

    awplus#

The six normal-mode lines are unchanged and the `SECURE-MODE POST` section is **empty**.
**Neither documented route to the cryptographic self-test information exists on this build.**
That is the finding, and it is the answer to the question this run was sent to settle.

### N11 — the PKI move `/flash/.certs -> /nvs/.certs`, and reversibility — **PASS. U3 ANSWERED: IT IS REVERSIBLE.**

**In secure mode (10:00):**

    Trustpoint "default-selfsigned"   RSA root  FAF19010 A6B8B53B 7F6D7CAC F7222476 C26B2633
                                      ECDSA root 169FD0D1 DEA6E435 187FD4F6 31D65681 7B916D67
    Trustpoint "default-system"       (present)
    Trustpoint "local"                ABSENT
    hostkeys: ecdsa 384 SHA256:l7MKU3gCfcN0IechQWU8zwxfkZ5ySwsVAAgMiyQwlls   (ONE key)

**After `no crypto secure-mode` + reload (10:07), against the fresh 09:37 baseline:**

    Trustpoint "default-selfsigned"   RSA root  084B6549 62DB51AD 493DD993 4353370C FC88C5DD   IDENTICAL
                                      ECDSA root 59915621 05FBC4FB D1D63A5E 3AAE2EE2 2D40E6C6  IDENTICAL
    Trustpoint "default-system"       present                                                  IDENTICAL
    Trustpoint "local"                BACK: RSA  C29CD492 253DF5BD F849819E F12866D8 792C90BE  IDENTICAL
                                            ECDSA 906D4096 FB243C13 F727C758 7F41DF0D CBCF1FB5 IDENTICAL
                                      server keys server-default / server-default-ecdsa        IDENTICAL
    hostkeys: rsa     3072 SHA256:iWG4XK1CWjkpR9bQM5MBAuYtiyt/PyBvY3yollNf54E                  IDENTICAL
              ecdsa    384 SHA256:oer/uwNjbwLIMUamUN5UW92BuJA81Cyh6ScF/x6RSrc                  IDENTICAL
              ed25519  256 SHA256:VhI6K5ajJHpjKXJ/vCsQSZPvEnlxxrOeMkrZRDXMgq4                  IDENTICAL

🔴 **READ THIS BEFORE YOU PANIC AT A MID-RUN PKI READ.** A PKI inventory taken **inside** secure
mode describes the **secure-mode store**, not the flash store. It looks exactly like
catastrophic loss — trustpoint `local` gone, two of three hostkeys gone, `default-selfsigned`
apparently regenerated — and it is not. The move is a genuine **move**: the flash store is set
aside intact and a separate secure-mode store with a single ECDSA hostkey is used instead, and
leaving secure mode restores the original byte-for-byte. **This run raised a false alarm on
exactly that mistake and had to correct it.** Do not grade the flash store from a secure-mode
read.

This matters beyond the PKI: trustpoint `local` carries the CA
`/O=Allied Telesis, Inc./CN=AlliedWarePlusCAA2WQS700B` that **T44270's PEAP TLS tunnel and
T9032's EAP-TLS server chain both passed off** in this same campaign. Had the move not been
reversible, enabling secure mode would have silently invalidated the credential two passed
cases depend on, and EAP-TLS and PEAP on this unit would stop working until it was rebuilt. It
is reversible, so they are intact — but that is the stake, and it is why N11 was measured on
both sides of the round trip rather than once.

🟢 **FINDING F-7 — one thing did NOT come back, and that is good news.** The `cktls` enrolment
under trustpoint `local` — created by T9032 run 1, with **no CLI to un-enroll it** on this
build, and which the queue forbade removing because that would mean deleting trustpoint `local`
— is **GONE**:

    before : Local RADIUS server user enrollments ready for export
                cktls
    after  : (the block is absent; the trustpoint and both certificate chains are back)

**A secure-mode round trip clears that residue without touching the trustpoint.** The thing no
CLI could delete was deleted by enabling and disabling secure mode. swi_d is cleaner after this
case than before it.

⚠️ **Residue left behind, minor:** `dir nvs:` after the round trip shows a **350-byte `hostkey`
file dated Oct 27 2026 20:53:11** — the secure-mode ECDSA hostkey, created at the secure-mode
boot and **not removed** when secure mode was disabled. It is device-generated key material; it
was deliberately not deleted. It is inert while secure mode is off and would presumably be
reused on a future enable.

### N12 — `radius-server local` withdrawn — **PASS** at config and wire level; the 802.1X half **NOT RUN**

The dispatch gave a choice: rebuild a rig so N12 has a subject, or drop it and say so. A
**minimal** local RADIUS server was rebuilt (§6f) — the full group A 802.1X rig was not.

| leg | normal mode | secure mode |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Access-Request from the box (`radsend.py`) | `<- Access-Accept id=21 in 0.001s authenticator=VALID (responder holds the secret)`, exit 0 | `<- NO REPLY after 5.0s (x1) [ICMP port unreachable: nothing listening on that UDP port]`, exit 1 |
| `show radius local-server user` | `Total users: 1 ... ckrad` | returns nothing at all |
| `radius-server local` in config mode | accepted | `% radius-server local is not supported in secure mode.` |
| the configured block | 5 lines in running-config | removed at the restart, announced in the boot warning list |

The **ICMP port-unreachable** is the strongest available "nothing is listening" — stronger than
silence, because it proves the host was reachable and the UDP port was closed. **PASS.**

⚠️ **FINDING F-8 — the refused command still changes CLI mode.** `radius-server local` printed
its refusal and the prompt nevertheless became `awplus(config-radsrv)#`:

    awplus(config)#radius-server local
    % radius-server local is not supported in secure mode.
    ...
    awplus(config-radsrv)#end

The sub-mode was entered although the command was rejected. Nothing was created (the subsequent
running-config has no `radius-server` line at all), so the effect is cosmetic — but a script
that gates its next command on the prompt would be misled, and this repo's own hard rule is to
gate each step on proven state.

🔴 **NOT RUN — the 802.1X half: "and what that does to the rig on port1.0.48".** The group A rig
was removed by T9032 run 2 earlier the same morning (§3a), so there was nothing on port1.0.48 to
break. Rebuilding the full `aaa authentication dot1x` + `dot1x port-control` + supplicant leg
was out of this case's scope and would have re-created state another case had just deliberately
torn down. **The wire-level RADIUS measurement above is the substitute, and it is a stronger
instrument for the row itself** (it proves the server stopped answering, which is the mechanism
the 802.1X failure would merely be a symptom of). The supplicant-level consequence remains
unmeasured and is named here so it is not read as covered.

### N13 — TACACS+, licensing, FIPS provider, `security_banner` — **MOSTLY NOT CONFIRMED**

| wiki row | verdict |
| --- | --- |
| TACACS+ groups not allowed | 🔴 **NOT DISCRIMINABLE.** `aaa group server ?` offers only `ldap` and `radius` in **normal** mode (§6e) — there is no `tacacs+` keyword on this build to withdraw (consistent with `platforms/x960.md` §7E.7). Control: `aaa group server radius ckg2` was **accepted** in secure mode, so RADIUS groups are untouched |
| licence activation not allowed | 🔴 **NOT CONFIRMED.** `license ?` in exec is **byte-identical** in both modes: `WORD / certificate / delete / redistribute / update`. An actual activation was **not attempted**: installing or removing a licence is the Test Engineer's (STANDING-ORDERS §4), and a missing feature is UNSUPPORTED, never something the tester installs around |
| FIPS becomes the default crypto provider | 🔴 **NOT MEASURABLE on this build.** No CLI surface exposes the crypto provider: `show crypto ?` offers only `key` and `pki`, in both modes. There is no `show crypto provider`, and `crypto secure-mode-selftest` — the command that would have reported it — does not exist (§N10) |
| `security_banner` configured on first enable | 🔴 **NOT CONFIRMED — it was not configured.** This unit starts from `no banner motd` (§5f), which is the cleanest possible subject. After the enable AND after the secure-mode restart, running-config **still read `no banner motd`**, and the post-reboot console login presented a bare `awplus login:` with no banner text whatsoever (box-side transcript). `show banner` is `% Incomplete command` and `show banner motd` is not a command on this build |

### N14 — console login still works afterwards — **PASS. U4 ANSWERED: YES.**

The one unknown that could have cost the bench the unit. Run 1 could only exclude the *known*
RADIUS mechanism; this is the real test. Every console session after the secure-mode restart
logged in from scratch — the driver logs in on each call — and reached a privileged prompt.
**Ten separate logins** between 09:54 and 10:04 with secure mode enabled, including the fresh
login immediately after the reboot (`login seen after 61 s`). Confirmed explicitly before
logging out:

    awplus#show privilege
    Current privilege level is 15

No login banner, no password-change demand, no delay. `aaa authentication login default local`
with a local `username manager privilege 15` is unaffected by secure mode, exactly as §6.6a of
run 1 predicted.

### N15 — `crypto secure-mode delete hostkey` — **DELIBERATELY NOT RUN**

Named in run 1's table precisely so it is not run by accident: it removes key material and is
nobody's smoke test. It was confirmed to exist, read-only, with `qmark` in **both** modes:

    awplus#crypto secure-mode ?
      delete  Remove key material

It was never executed. This is a deliberate non-execution, not a limb that could not be reached.


8. THE FOUR TITLE CLAUSES, ANSWERED PLAINLY
--------------------------------------------

The case title is *"Disable Telnet, SNMPv1/v2, all privilege levels except 1 and 15, weak crypto
(MD5, RSA1, DSA)"*. In plain English, for a reader who wants one line each:

| clause | does secure mode do it on this unit? | how it was proved |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Disable Telnet** | **YES**, the server. The client is NOT disabled (F-2) | a telnet login that worked became `Connection refused` (a TCP RST) |
| **Disable SNMPv1/v2** | **YES** | an SNMP GET that returned the software version stopped answering, and the community was deleted from the configuration |
| **All privilege levels except 1 and 15** | **YES** | privilege 7 refused by name, privilege 1 accepted in the same breath, and the existing privilege-7 user deleted at boot |
| **Weak crypto (MD5, RSA1, DSA)** | **MD5 yes. RSA1 and DSA: not applicable** (F-4) | NTP MD5 and SNMPv3 MD5 both refused by name, SHA1/SHA-256 accepted. RSA1 and DSA cannot be generated on this build in EITHER mode, so secure mode withdraws nothing |

And one clause the title does not mention but secure mode enforces **more** strictly than its own
documentation: **SHA-1 is refused too** (F-3), so SNMPv3 on this unit means SHA-256 or nothing.


9. FINDINGS — the eight things worth a developer's time
---------------------------------------------------------

Each is stated as a finding in its own right, not as an aside, and each names the evidence.

| # | finding | evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **F-1** | **`show secure-mode` reads "enabled" before secure mode is in force.** Between the enable and the restart the device reports the feature on while only the telnet server is actually stopped — SNMPv2c, local RADIUS and the whole CLI parser are untouched. The `show` describes the configuration, not the enforced state | §N1/§N3(i): `show secure-mode` = enabled at 09:50:27, while snmpq returned a value and radsend got Access-Accept at 09:50:5x |
| **F-2** | **CR-84010 is not implemented: the telnet CLIENT still runs in secure mode.** The wiki says the client is disabled. It opened a socket and received a RST from the far end | §N4: `devclient.py`, `CLIENT RESULT: REFUSED`, and `telnet ?` still offered in exec |
| **F-3** | **Secure mode is stricter than its own documentation: SHA-1 is refused for SNMPv3, not just MD5 and DES.** Only SHA-256 is accepted. A conforming SNMPv3 user built per the wiki is silently deleted at the next restart | §N5: `% SHA-1 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode`, and the boot-time rejection of the existing `auth sha` user |
| **F-4** | **The "rsa1 and dsa not supported" row cannot be confirmed here, because they are absent in NORMAL mode too.** `crypto key generate hostkey ?` offers only ecdsa/ed25519/rsa in both modes; rsa1 and dsa appear only in the `crypto key destroy userkey` path, in both modes | §6e and §N7, two qmark probes either side of the enable |
| **F-5** | 🔴 **The CLI help surface does not reflect ANY secure-mode restriction.** Every probed prefix returns byte-identical completions in both modes — `username ... privilege ?` still offers `<0-15>`, `ntp authentication-key N ?` still offers `md5`, `snmp-server user ... auth ?` still offers `md5`, `service telnet ?` still offers `ip`, `ssh server ?` still offers `allow-legacy-ssh-rsa`, and `show running-config ?` still lists `unencrypted`. Every one of those is then rejected when actually typed. **The help offers what the parser forbids** | §6e vs §N7/§N8/§N10: three qmark runs in normal mode and three in secure mode |
| **F-6** | **Neither documented route to the cryptographic self-test exists.** `crypto secure-mode-selftest` is `% Unrecognized command` in secure mode as well as normal mode, and `crypto ?` gains no new sub-command; `show post` adds a `SECURE-MODE POST` heading with **nothing under it** | §N10, settled from the box-side transcript |
| **F-7** | 🟢 **A secure-mode round trip deletes a PKI enrolment that no CLI can remove.** The `cktls` enrolment under trustpoint `local`, un-removable on this build, is gone after enable+disable while the trustpoint and both certificate chains return intact | §N11, before/after `show crypto pki certificates local` |
| **F-8** | **A refused command still changes CLI mode.** `radius-server local` prints `% radius-server local is not supported in secure mode.` and the prompt becomes `awplus(config-radsrv)#` anyway | §N12, verbatim transcript |

Two further rows of the wiki were **not confirmed** and are reported as such rather than as
passes: `security_banner` was **not** configured on first enable (§N13), and licence activation
showed **no** change in its CLI surface (§N13).


10. ⚠️ METHOD WARNINGS FOR THE NEXT TESTER
-------------------------------------------

1. 🔴 **Do not grade the PKI from a read taken inside secure mode.** It describes the
   secure-mode store, looks exactly like catastrophic key loss, and is not. This run raised a
   false alarm to the Test Engineer on precisely that mistake and had to correct it fifteen
   minutes later. Read the flash store **after** leaving secure mode (§N11).
2. 🔴 **`tools/tb probe --template` resolves the path ON THE BOX.** The dev-host path and the
   box path for this same tree differ. A wrong path prints `no template ... -- diff skipped`
   and **still exits 0**, which is indistinguishable from MATCH at a glance (§4.4).
3. 🔴 **A deliberate reboot guarantees a DIRTY health verdict.** `tools/tb health --before`
   flags the ~34 ordinary kernel boot messages (`Linux version`, `KASLR disabled`,
   `mv_xor_v2 ... XOR driver`) because they match `kernel`. They are not crashes. Attribute them
   to the reboot you caused rather than reporting a fault (§12).
4. **`tools/snmpq.py` can report `RESULT VALUE` exit 0 for a net-snmp usage error.** Check the
   `CMD` line and the `|` output before trusting a VALUE (§N5).
5. **The probe's `CHECK_CABLE` lines on this bench are FDB ageing, and one ping clears them.**
   Settled definitively here: the after-probe read exit 2 with eth3 and eth4 `CHECK_CABLE`, two
   `tools/tb ping` calls refreshed the FDB, and the re-probe read **exit 0 MATCH** (§11). Run 1
   could only infer this; it is now reproducible.
6. **`crypto secure-mode` needs no answer and no `YN?:`.** It prompts for nothing. Drive it with
   `ckcon --verbatim`, not `ckyn`.


11. AFTER THE RUN — teardown, restore and the probe
-----------------------------------------------------

The recovery ladder of run 1 §8 was followed in order. **It stopped at rung 1.** Rung 2
(`boot config-file flash:/pre-securemode.cfg`) and rung 3 (the both-outlet power cut) were never
needed and were not used.

**Rung 1, as written.** First this run's own secure-mode-era additions were removed
(`no username ckp1`, `no ntp authentication-key 11`, `no snmp-server user ckv3`,
`no snmp-server group ckgrp`, `no snmp-server view ckview`,
`no ssh server disallow-cbc-ciphers`, `no aaa group server radius ckg2` — all accepted, cfg.py
exit 0). Then:

    awplus(config)#no crypto secure-mode
    % Save the config and restart the system for this change to take effect.
    awplus#write
    Building configuration...
    [OK]
    awplus#show secure-mode
    Secure mode is disabled
    awplus#show startup-config | include secure
    (nothing)

`reload` → `reboot system? (y/n): y` → **login seen after 60 s**. After the restart:
`show secure-mode` = `Secure mode is disabled`; `show boot` =
`Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)` — **the gate-7 baseline, unchanged
throughout**; `dir nvs:` works again.

**Configuration restored — the authoritative diff.** The restored running-config was compared
against run 1's `swi_d.cfg` as committed in git at `c354205`, i.e. the true pre-case baseline:

    the ONLY differences are the group A 802.1X lines:
      - radius-server host 127.0.0.1 key <encrypted>
      - aaa authentication dot1x default group radius
      - radius-server local / server enable / nas 127.0.0.1 / user ckmulti1,ckmulti2,ckpeap,cktls
      - dot1x port-control auto  and  dot1x control-direction both  on port1.0.48

🔴 **Those lines were removed by T9032 run 2 at 09:26 this morning, BEFORE run 2 of this case
started** (§3a), and their absence was recorded in run 2's own pre-run capture at 09:46. **Run 2
did not remove them and has not failed to restore them.** Against the state it actually
inherited, the restore is **exact**: no other line differs.

**PKI restored** — all three hostkey fingerprints and all three trustpoints byte-identical to
the 09:37 baseline, with the single intended exception of the `cktls` enrolment (F-7). Full
comparison in §N11.

**Flash restored.** Both files this run created were deleted and the deletion proved:

    awplus#delete flash:/pre-securemode.cfg   ->  Delete flash:/pre-securemode.cfg?  (y/n)[n]:y
                                                  Deleting... Successful operation
    awplus#delete flash:/pre-sm-rig.cfg       ->  Deleting... Successful operation
    awplus#dir flash:/pre-sm*                 ->  % No such file or directory

⚠️ Left behind deliberately: `nvs:hostkey`, 350 bytes, the secure-mode ECDSA hostkey the device
created at its own secure-mode boot. Device-generated key material; not this run's to delete
(§N11).

**Probe, after.** The first after-probe read **exit 2 NEEDS-CHECK** with `CHECK_CABLE` on eth3
and eth4 — the same artefact run 1 reported, now explained and cleared rather than merely
described. The DUT side was read directly first:

    awplus#show interface port1.0.10 status
    port1.0.10  -   connected   10  a-full  a-1000  1000BASE-T
    awplus#show interface port1.0.48 status
    port1.0.48  -   connected   48  a-full  a-1000  1000BASE-T
    awplus#show arp
    10.38.22.33   000d.b955.770e   vlan10   port1.0.10   dynamic

Both ports connected; `tools/tb ping tb277 eth3 10.38.22.34 2` → 0% loss. The cause is the FDB:
this is an all-console case that sends no traffic, `--read-only` suppresses the probe's own
host-NIC pings, and **the two reboots flushed the FDB outright**. After one ping on eth3 and one
on eth4 the probe was re-run with identical facts:

    MATCH -- the bench equals the template.                    exit 0

🟢 **Final bench state: exit 0 MATCH.** Run 1's §9 left this as an inference; it is now proved.

**`bench-setup/tb277/bench-state.md`** — the probe regenerates this shared file and a
one-console probe reduces it to swi_d only. It was reverted and **not** committed by this run.
See §13 for the separate matter of what HEAD already contained.

**Console.** Read from the box-side transcript (`/tmp/secmode2/console-u2.log`), never from a
tool's stdout:

    awplus#
    >>> show secure-mode
    Secure mode is disabled
    awplus#
    >>> show privilege
    Current privilege level is 15
    awplus#
    >>> exit
    exit

    awplus login:
    ===== closed /dev/u2 at 2026-10-28 10:13:22 =====

Left at the **login prompt, logged out** — not in config mode, not at a shell, not holding a
session. `end` was issued first and `terminal no monitor` is handled by the driver from exec.

**Processes.** This run started nothing persistent: no framework run, no capture, no traffic
generator, no watcher. Nothing to stop. `/tmp/secmode2/` on the box holds the tools copy, the
console transcript and the two health samples, and is left in place as evidence.

**Consoles u3 and u5 were never opened**, and the `~/.ckenv` credential gate was never
exercised.


