14. VERDICT
------------

Run label: **clean.** Two reboots, both deliberate, both attributable; no unexpected reboot; no
crash; the exception log unchanged from a five-day-old pre-existing entry. Not confounded.

Per-limb summary of §7:

| | limbs |
| --- | --- |
| **measured and confirmed** (11) | N1 enable, N2 reboot behaviour (U1), N3 startup-config (U2), N4 telnet server, N5 SNMPv1/v2c disabled, N6 privilege levels, N7 NTP MD5 + SSH legacy-rsa, N8 `show run unencrypted`, N9 NVS, N11 PKI move and its reversal (U3), N14 console login (U4) |
| **product findings** (4) | F-2 telnet client not disabled, F-3 SHA-1 refused beyond the documentation, F-6 no self-test by either route, F-8 refused command still changes mode — plus F-1 and F-5 as cross-cutting `show`/help defects, and F-7 as a benign one |
| **not confirmed** (2) | `security_banner` not configured on first enable; licence activation surface unchanged |
| **not discriminable on this build** (3) | rsa1/dsa (absent in normal mode too), TACACS+ groups (absent in normal mode too), `enable password` ≥ 8 (a pre-existing `security-password minimum-length 8` produces the same refusal) |
| **not measurable from this bench** (2) | an SNMPv3 GET under secure mode (net-snmp 5.7.3 + broken pysnmp, both root to fix); the FIPS crypto provider (no CLI surface exposes it) |
| **not run, with reason** (3) | N12's 802.1X supplicant half (the rig was removed by T9032 run 2 before this window; the wire-level RADIUS measurement substitutes); DES-alone as an SNMPv3 privacy protocol (the line was rejected on its auth keyword first); licence activation itself (STANDING-ORDERS §4 — the Test Engineer's) |
| **deliberately not run** (1) | N15 `crypto secure-mode delete hostkey` — key material, named in run 1 precisely so it is never a smoke test |

**All four clauses of the case title were driven and confirmed** (§8): telnet disabled proved by
a TCP RST where a login worked before; SNMPv1/v2c disabled proved by a GET that stopped
answering; privilege levels restricted to 1 and 15 proved by a refusal and an accepted control
in the same breath; MD5 refused proved in two independent features with SHA controls accepted.
RSA1 and DSA are the one clause secure mode cannot be credited with, because this build never
offered them (F-4).

The enable limb and its recovery — the entire reason this case was PARTIAL — are complete. The
unit was taken into secure mode, measured there, and brought back to its exact starting state,
with the recovery stopping at the first rung.

VERDICT: PASS -- secure mode enforces all four clauses of the case title, each proved at command
or wire level against a normal-mode control, and the unit was fully restored; eight findings
recorded, of which F-2 (telnet client still enabled), F-3 (SHA-1 refused beyond the
documentation), F-5 (CLI help reflects no restriction at all) and F-6 (neither route to the
cryptographic self-test exists) are product deviations worth a developer's time; nine named
limbs are not-confirmed, not-discriminable, not-measurable or not-run, each with its reason.
